From 8c7646d6d3300bcba9b9363fa2253493bd552850 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 07:11:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(kanban): never pin a pickup-stall alarm to the
 stalled machine

t_01a1ce5f6ddc72f6. The pickup-stall detector filed every alarm with runs_on=[machine:<own>]. <own> is the
machine taking no Board work (a registry hold, or no spawn slot: budget_exhausted), so the alarm sat ready
until the condition cleared. "Pickup held on worker-2" (t_01a1ce40aaa4652e) went unclaimed for the whole
05:34Z hold. On 07:21Z, worker-6's queue-full alarm t_01a1cea1a109b34f took worker-6's last slot, ahead of
the card it reported.

- New `alarm_placement(own, registry)` is the only path. It never returns machine:<own>. In order:
  1. Another active, unheld, pickup-advertising machine that is eligible now: it carries the alarm's
     capability:{terminal,file,kanban} and profile:<assignee>, plus hub access (it is the hub, or it
     has capability:hub-write), it is not draining, it was seen in the last 300 s, and it has a free
     card slot. The one with the most free slots wins.
  2. The hub operator lane.
  3. Any other host, ignoring capacity.
  4. Another held machine (hub first).
  5. Default placement when no other machine copies cards.
  With the registry unreadable: machine:hub, or, on the hub, a label set the hub does not carry.
- An open, unclaimed covering alarm whose pin is stranded is re-pinned once, with a detector comment.
  Stranded means it names <own>, or a machine that can no longer host it. Legacy alarms are fixed on
  the next sweep. Running and claimed alarms are never touched. A healthy pin is never churned.
- The body now carries <own>'s machine-local evidence, because the assignee is elsewhere: the
  pickup-skip row for each listed target, <own>'s registry entry (hold, hold_by/at/reason, slots) and
  <own>'s replica path. It no longer says "on this host".
- Detector thresholds, grouping and hold handling are unchanged. Nothing lifts or shortens a hold.

Proof:
- RED: on the old module, test_kanban_pickup_alarm_placement.py fails 26 of 28 tests. The hold and
  budget_exhausted end-to-end tests fail with `['machine:worker-1'] == ['machine:worker-2']`, and the
  hub-claim test with `['machine:worker-1'] == ['machine:hub']`. GREEN: alarm_placement +
  pickup_stall + worker_node suites pass, 120 tests.
- A fake-Board end-to-end test shows M held, the alarm pinned machine:hub, and the hub's pickup
  imports and leases it (status running). M still imports nothing.
- Mutation: 30 of 30 mutants of the new conditions are killed. That covers every _can_host,
  _ready_now and fallback branch, plus the no-registry rules, _stranded, the _replace_stranded
  guards, the create pin and the body evidence. None of them was a syntax error.
- Read-only replay (mode=ro) on 2026-11-12T07:21Z covers all 6 Board replicas on worker-5 (askeve,
  bb, default, hermes, runners, worker-load). Each replica holds every gateway's rows. It took every
  open pickup-stall-detector card plus every one created in the last 7 days: 131 alarms (6 open), filed by
  worker-2 89, hub 27, worker-1 8, worker-3 5, worker-4 1 and worker-6 1. By cause: 112 card-specific,
  12 budget_exhausted, 5 hold and 2 evidence_missing. The old placement pinned 131 of 131 to the
  stalled machine (125 still recorded that way). The new placement pins 0 of 131 to it, and 131 of 131
  to a machine eligible now under the live registry, with the alarm's own machine forced held or full
  per its cause: worker-3 126, worker-5 5. The 2 open hub alarms still ready on machine:hub
  (t_01a1ce74a19037ce, t_01a1ce8d5431ef29) would go to worker-3.
---
